But, you know, I don't see the texting can be a very good way to establish a connection. It's not necessarily the best way to create the optimal connection. But if you use it a place to start, that's okay. How has your research changed the way you live your own life? It has been foundational. I really remember when I got off the train that morning when I talked to the woman in the red hat, I thought, I do this a lot.

53
00:30:50.000 --> 00:31:23.000
I would never have called myself a horribly introverted person, but, you know, I was horribly terrified of stage fright when I was younger when I was in grad school, terrified of public speaking then, nervous at conferences to talk with people. You know, had my head down. walking around a lot. I was a college football player in a Division III college football player at St. Olaf, um, yeah. And, and so, you know, I was kind of rough and kind of quiet, I would say, at times. I could be, avoidant, like to spend a lot of time in the woods. And it, this has really

54
00:31:23.000 --> 00:31:54.000
changed the way I live, live my life kind of top to bottom. And a few fundamental things. One is I take an interest in other people in a way I never did before, and to a degree I never did before. because I know how interesting anybody can be. Bill Nye, famous science guy, Cornell grad. I got my PhD from there, go big red. Bill Nye, once famous, he said, everyone you ever meet knows something you don't. He's right about that, right?

55
00:31:54.000 --> 00:32:16.000
I also have learned from our data that people are exceptionally kind, can be very kind if you reach out and give them the opportunity to. So I give people the opportunity to do that. It's made a difference in how I interact, with strangers, my train rides are almost never silent anymore. I've turned tons of strangers into acquaintances, even if just for a moment, you know, just for a little bit, made those moments

56
00:32:16.000 --> 00:32:46.000
better. My friendships have gotten stronger because I'm more open. I think about others more, reach out a little more often. When I know somebody is in need of support, I don't think I got to fix something. I reach out and tell my thinking about you. I care about you. And I will reach out again tomorrow and connect with you then. And I'll stop by and take them down to coffee. Whatever. I will, I reach out more often. In my marriage, I would say I'm more open and honest. I'm less defensive in conversation. Reach out, I think have better conversations with my wife.

57
00:32:46.000 --> 00:33:17.000
And it's even affected some major decisions that I've been involved with in our lives. We lost a baby 10 years ago this year. July 11th, 2016, six months into my wife's pregnancy, our daughter who we named Sophie, had Down syndrome. We were prepared to go forward. We're very excited for this baby. We'd already had four other children adopted to over the years, but this was going to be a whole new adventure in our lives.

58
00:33:17.000 --> 00:33:38.000
And when we lost her, it was super painful. And it was about a year after that, when Jen, my wife, she was sitting in the sunroom one morning, and I asked her if she would, you know, if she might consider adopting a child again. Because we were ready to go. We could maybe do this again. And she asked me,

59
00:33:38.000 --> 00:34:05.000
it turns out we can adopt a child with Down syndrome. Would you be willing to do that? Could we consider adopting a child with Down syndrome? And my head was not in that place at that time. That's not where my mind was. But at that time, as every researcher who listens to your podcast can attest, their data enters into their thinking at times. And this comes up for me all the time.

60
00:34:05.000 --> 00:34:38.000
It lives in my head all the time. And right there, I felt it was just clear to me. My wife was giving me that the choice we all have to reach out and engage with somebody or to hold back and avoid them. And I'd seen thousands and thousands and thousands of data points of people being overly pessimistic about how well it would go when they reached out to connect with another person. And I realized that in that moment, I was doing exactly the same thing. And it gave me some courage to say, yeah, we can do this.

61
00:34:38.000 --> 00:35:22.000
We can do this. And, you know, because Jen and I were in such a good place and she was on board, we were on board with us together, it gave me the courage to go forward. We brought Lindsay, our amazing 10-year-old daughter home eight years ago now. We adapted her in 2018 from China, and she is just all shades of fabulous. Not always easy. Hard, hard raising child at times, of course, one with intellectual disabilities, too. But holy cow, has she blessed our lives in ways that I just never could have imagined. So it is profoundly changed my life in ways both moment to moment day to day and massive. That's a great story.

62
00:35:22.000 --> 00:35:48.000
Well, let me close by asking you what you're working on now. I mean, what are the big questions that you're trying to answer today? So I'm interested in, one, why is it we don't learn this? Why don't we figure this out over time? Avoidance is one, but Stavatier, one of my wonderful postdocs, who's now at the University of Wisconsin-Madison, and I learned recently. We just published a paper in APA's Journal of Personality and Social Psychology Journal.

63
00:35:48.000 --> 00:36:14.000
We learned that even after people have a conversation that goes well, an interaction that goes well, they do learn from that. They think another conversation would be great, but a couple weeks after that, when they go back to have another conversation, all of the learning that they had two weeks ago is gone. And now they're right back where they were. So the misunderstanding seems persistent, and that's interesting to me. I'm also interested in the question we talked about before,

64
00:36:14.000 --> 00:36:46.000
which is variability around the world. Sociality looks different around the world in lots of ways. I think the effects we're documenting have a fundamental quality to admit, you know, everywhere on the planet it's possible for people to be overly pessimistic about how their interactions will go. to the extent that varies around the world, I don't know, to the extent that matters and explaining differences in sociality, I'm not sure yet. We're just starting on that. We certainly have looked at variance in expectations within cultures, people who are more pessimistic are actually more mistaken.

65
00:36:46.000 --> 00:37:28.000
And my bet is that's going on around the world too, but we don't know yet. So that's the other place that I'm going. And so I would say those are the two big things. And then we're also, we've also gotten interested thirdly and how costly this might be. How much time are we actually choosing not to spend with other people that we could? How much different could our lives actually be if we were calibrated about how others would respond when we reach out to them? So we're starting to think about this in more practical terms in that way. And what I'm confident about, though, is that new ideals will keep coming up about this because they do almost every week in conversations as my grad students.

